GRAMMAR_TOPICS: list[dict[str, Any]] = json.loads('[{"slug":"verb-to-be","title":"The verb \'to be\'","language":"en","grades":[5],"summary":"The verb \'to be\' describes identity, age, feelings, states and location.","explanation":"The verb \'to be\' describes identity, age, feelings, states and location. It is irregular, so its form changes with the subject. In the present, use am with I, is with he/she/it and singular nouns, and are with you/we/they and plural nouns. In the past, use was with I/he/she/it and were with you/we/they. English usually needs a verb in a sentence, even when German would not use one.","formation":["Present: I am; you are; he/she/it is; we/you/they are.","Negative: add not after the verb (is not / isn\'t; are not / aren\'t).","Questions: put the verb before the subject (Are you ready?).","Past: was / were; questions use Was/Were + subject."],"usage":["Introduce people and things.","Talk about age, feelings, weather, time, place and condition.","Ask and answer yes/no questions."],"examples":[{"sentence":"I am twelve years old.","note":"Use am with I."},{"sentence":"They aren\'t at home.","note":"The negative comes after are."},{"sentence":"Were you tired yesterday?","note":"Move were before the subject for a question."}],"exceptions":["Do not use do/does to form questions with to be.","In standard English, age is expressed with be: I am twelve, not I have twelve."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: She are happy. Correct: She is happy.","Incorrect: Do you are ready? Correct: Are you ready."],"related_topics":["present-simple","questions-word-order"],"exercises":[{"question":"My friends ___ in the classroom.","options":["is","are","am"],"answer":"are","explanation":"Use are with a plural subject."},{"question":"___ your brother at home?","options":["Is","Does","Are"],"answer":"Is","explanation":"A question with to be begins with the correct form of be."}]},{"slug":"have-got","title":"Have got / Has got","language":"en","grades":[5],"summary":"Have got is commonly used in British English to talk about possession, family relationships and features.","explanation":"Have got is commonly used in British English to talk about possession, family relationships and features. Use have got with I/you/we/they and has got with he/she/it. In questions, have or has moves before the subject. In negatives, add not to have or has. The form got does not change.","formation":["Affirmative: I/you/we/they have got; he/she/it has got.","Negative: haven\'t got / hasn\'t got.","Question: Have/Has + subject + got + noun?","Short answers: Yes, I have. / No, she hasn\'t."],"usage":["Talk about possessions and what people look like.","Describe family members, pets and things a person owns."],"examples":[{"sentence":"Mia has got two brothers.","note":"Use has got with she/Mia."},{"sentence":"Have you got a pencil?","note":"Have comes before the subject."},{"sentence":"We haven\'t got much time.","note":"The negative form is haven\'t got."}],"exceptions":["Have got is especially common in British English; American English often uses have.","Do not combine do-support with have got in standard forms: Have you got...?"],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: He have got a dog. Correct: He has got a dog.","Incorrect: Do you have got...? Correct: Have you got...?"],"related_topics":["verb-to-be","present-simple"],"exercises":[{"question":"Liam ___ got a new bike.","options":["have","has","is"],"answer":"has","explanation":"Use has got with he, she, it and singular names."},{"question":"___ they got a cat?","options":["Have","Has","Do"],"answer":"Have","explanation":"Use have before they in questions."}]},{"slug":"present-simple","title":"Present Simple","language":"en","grades":[5,6],"summary":"The Present Simple is used for routines, habits, facts and situations that are generally true.","explanation":"The Present Simple is used for routines, habits, facts and situations that are generally true. With I/you/we/they, use the base form of the verb. With he/she/it or a singular noun, add -s or -es. Questions and negatives usually use do or does; after does, the main verb returns to its base form.","formation":["Affirmative: I play; she plays.","Negative: subject + do/does not + base verb (don\'t play / doesn\'t play).","Question: Do/Does + subject + base verb?","Spelling: usually add -s; add -es after -s, -sh, -ch, -x and often -o; consonant + y becomes -ies."],"usage":["Habits and repeated actions: every day, often, usually.","Facts and general truths.","Timetables and stable states."],"examples":[{"sentence":"He plays tennis every Saturday.","note":"Add -s for he."},{"sentence":"I don\'t like coffee.","note":"Use don\'t + base form."},{"sentence":"Does your sister speak English?","note":"After does, use speak, not speaks."}],"exceptions":["Be, have and some other verbs have special forms.","Stative verbs such as know, believe and understand are not normally used in progressive forms for their usual meanings."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: She play football. Correct: She plays football.","Incorrect: Does he likes music? Correct: Does he like music."],"related_topics":["verb-to-be","present-progressive","adverbs-of-frequency"],"exercises":[{"question":"Ben usually ___ breakfast at seven.","options":["have","has","is having"],"answer":"has","explanation":"A singular third-person subject takes -s in the Present Simple."},{"question":"___ your parents work here?","options":["Does","Do","Are"],"answer":"Do","explanation":"Use do with a plural subject."}]},{"slug":"questions-word-order","title":"Questions and Word Order","language":"en","grades":[5,6],"summary":"English normally follows subject–verb–object order in statements.","explanation":"English normally follows subject–verb–object order in statements. In many questions, an auxiliary verb comes before the subject. Use do/does/did when there is no other auxiliary and the main verb is not to be. Put question words such as what, where, when, why, who and how at the beginning. In questions with be or a modal verb, move that verb before the subject instead of adding do.","formation":["Statement: Subject + verb + object (Tom likes music).","Present Simple question: Do/Does + subject + base verb?","Past Simple question: Did + subject + base verb?","Be/modal question: Are you ready? / Can she swim?","Question word + auxiliary + subject + main verb: Where do you live?"],"usage":["Ask for people, things, places, times, reasons and methods.","Build clear statements and questions in writing and speaking."],"examples":[{"sentence":"Where does your cousin live?","note":"Question word + does + subject + base verb."},{"sentence":"Why were they late?","note":"Move were before the subject."},{"sentence":"What did you buy?","note":"Use the base form after did."}],"exceptions":["Who can be the subject: Who called you? In this case, do-support is not used.","Indirect questions use statement word order: Do you know where she lives?"],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: Where you live? Correct: Where do you live?","Incorrect: Did you went? Correct: Did you go?"],"related_topics":["present-simple","past-simple","indirect-questions"],"exercises":[{"question":"Where ___ your best friend live?","options":["do","does","is"],"answer":"does","explanation":"Use does with a singular third-person subject."},{"question":"What did you ___ yesterday?","options":["saw","see","seen"],"answer":"see","explanation":"After did, use the base form."}]},{"slug":"articles-plurals","title":"Articles and Plural Nouns","language":"en","grades":[5,6],"summary":"Use a or an with a singular countable noun when it is not specific or when mentioning it for the first time.","explanation":"Use a or an with a singular countable noun when it is not specific or when mentioning it for the first time. Choose a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound. Use the when the listener knows which person or thing you mean. Many nouns form the plural with -s or -es, but some have irregular plural forms. Do not use a/an with plural nouns or uncountable nouns.","formation":["a + consonant sound: a book, a university.","an + vowel sound: an apple, an hour.","the + a specific or already-known noun.","Regular plural: book/books; box/boxes; city/cities.","Irregular plurals: child/children; person/people; man/men."],"usage":["Introduce a singular countable noun.","Refer to a known or specific person or thing.","Talk about more than one item."],"examples":[{"sentence":"I saw a dog. The dog was brown.","note":"Use a when introducing it, then the for the same dog."},{"sentence":"She ate an orange.","note":"Orange begins with a vowel sound."},{"sentence":"Two children are playing.","note":"Child has an irregular plural."}],"exceptions":["Article choice depends on sound, not just spelling: an hour but a university.","Some nouns have the same singular and plural form, for example sheep."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: an university. Correct: a university.","Incorrect: two childs. Correct: two children."],"related_topics":["countable-uncountable","pronouns-determiners"],"exercises":[{"question":"She has ___ umbrella.","options":["a","an","the"],"answer":"an","explanation":"Umbrella begins with a vowel sound."},{"question":"One child, three ___.","options":["childs","childes","children"],"answer":"children","explanation":"Children is the irregular plural of child."}]},{"slug":"adverbs-of-frequency","title":"Adverbs of Frequency","language":"en","grades":[5,6],"summary":"Adverbs of frequency describe how often something happens.","explanation":"Adverbs of frequency describe how often something happens. Common examples are always, usually, often, sometimes, rarely and never. In neutral statements, they usually go before the main verb, but after the verb be. With auxiliary verbs, they usually go after the first auxiliary. Their position can change for emphasis, especially sometimes and usually.","formation":["Before a main verb: I usually walk to school.","After be: She is often tired after training.","After the first auxiliary: We have never visited Canada.","With negatives: I don\'t usually watch TV in the morning.","Frequency scale: always (100%) → usually → often → sometimes → rarely → never (0%)."],"usage":["Describe routines and habits.","Say how often an event happens and compare routines."],"examples":[{"sentence":"My brother often plays chess after school.","note":"The adverb comes before the main verb plays."},{"sentence":"They are sometimes late.","note":"The adverb comes after the verb be."},{"sentence":"I have never eaten sushi.","note":"The adverb comes after the auxiliary have."}],"exceptions":["Sometimes can also go at the beginning or end: Sometimes we walk; We walk sometimes.","Never already has a negative meaning, so do not normally add not: I never go there."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: She plays always tennis. Correct: She always plays tennis.","Incorrect: He never doesn\'t listen. Correct: He never listens / He doesn\'t ever listen."],"related_topics":["present-simple","present-progressive"],"exercises":[{"question":"I ___ brush my teeth before bed. It is my routine.","options":["usually","am usually","usually am"],"answer":"usually","explanation":"An adverb of frequency normally comes before the main verb."},{"question":"Maya is ___ late; she is always on time.","options":["never","ever never","not never"],"answer":"never","explanation":"Never expresses zero frequency and is not normally combined with not."}]},{"slug":"present-progressive","title":"Present Progressive / Present Continuous","language":"en","grades":[6,7],"summary":"The Present Progressive describes an action happening around the time of speaking or a temporary situation.","explanation":"The Present Progressive describes an action happening around the time of speaking or a temporary situation. It is formed with the present form of be and the -ing form of the main verb. The spelling of the -ing form sometimes changes: drop a final silent -e, double a final consonant in some short stressed words, or change -ie to -y.","formation":["Affirmative: am/is/are + verb-ing.","Negative: am/is/are + not + verb-ing.","Question: Am/Is/Are + subject + verb-ing?","Spelling: make → making; run → running; lie → lying."],"usage":["Actions happening now or around now.","Temporary situations and changing trends.","Arrangements in the near future when a time is clear."],"examples":[{"sentence":"I am reading a book at the moment.","note":"The action is happening now."},{"sentence":"We are staying with our aunt this week.","note":"This is temporary."},{"sentence":"She is meeting us at six.","note":"Present Progressive can describe an arrangement."}],"exceptions":["Stative verbs such as know, need and own are usually not progressive in their ordinary meanings.","Some verbs change meaning: I think it is true (opinion), but I am thinking about it (process)."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: They playing. Correct: They are playing.","Incorrect: I am know him. Correct: I know him."],"related_topics":["present-simple","past-progressive","future-forms"],"exercises":[{"question":"Look! The baby ___.","options":["cries","is crying","cry"],"answer":"is crying","explanation":"Use the Present Progressive for an action happening now."},{"question":"We ___ dinner at the moment.","options":["are having","have","has"],"answer":"are having","explanation":"Use are + -ing with we."}]},{"slug":"present-simple-vs-progressive","title":"Present Simple or Present Progressive?","language":"en","grades":[6,7],"summary":"Choose the Present Simple for routines, repeated actions and general truths.","explanation":"Choose the Present Simple for routines, repeated actions and general truths. Choose the Present Progressive for actions in progress, temporary situations and developments. Time expressions can help, but meaning is more important than a signal word. Compare \'I work in London\' (generally/stably) with \'I am working in London this month\' (temporarily).","formation":["Present Simple: base verb; add -s/-es with he/she/it.","Present Progressive: am/is/are + verb-ing.","Check the meaning: routine or fact versus temporary action or change."],"usage":["Describe what people usually do versus what they are doing now.","Contrast permanent and temporary situations."],"examples":[{"sentence":"She walks to school every day.","note":"A regular routine."},{"sentence":"She is walking to school today because her bike is broken.","note":"A temporary situation today."},{"sentence":"The climate is getting warmer.","note":"A change or development."}],"exceptions":["Always can be used with the Progressive to express annoyance: He is always losing his keys.","Signal words are clues, not absolute rules."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: I am going to school every day (for a routine). Better: I go to school every day.","Incorrect: Listen! She sings. Better: Listen! She is singing."],"related_topics":["present-simple","present-progressive"],"exercises":[{"question":"My dad usually ___ at 7 a.m.","options":["gets up","is getting up","get up"],"answer":"gets up","explanation":"Usually describes a routine."},{"question":"Be quiet! I ___.","options":["study","am studying","studies"],"answer":"am studying","explanation":"The action is happening now."}]},{"slug":"past-simple","title":"Past Simple","language":"en","grades":[6,7],"summary":"The Past Simple describes completed actions and states in the past, often with a finished time such as yesterday, last year or two days ago.","explanation":"The Past Simple describes completed actions and states in the past, often with a finished time such as yesterday, last year or two days ago. Regular verbs usually end in -ed. Irregular verbs have special forms that must be learned. For negatives and most questions, use did/didn\'t followed by the base form of the main verb.","formation":["Affirmative: subject + past form (played / went).","Negative: subject + did not/didn\'t + base verb.","Question: Did + subject + base verb?","Be: was/were; questions use Was/Were + subject."],"usage":["Completed past events and sequences of actions.","Past habits and states that are no longer current.","Stories with a finished past time reference."],"examples":[{"sentence":"We visited York last summer.","note":"A completed action at a finished time."},{"sentence":"He didn\'t see the message.","note":"Use didn\'t + base verb."},{"sentence":"Did they arrive on time?","note":"Use did + subject + base verb."}],"exceptions":["Irregular verbs include go/went, have/had, take/took and buy/bought.","The verb be does not use did in ordinary questions or negatives."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: She didn\'t went. Correct: She didn\'t go.","Incorrect: Did you watched it? Correct: Did you watch it?"],"related_topics":["past-progressive","past-perfect","reported-speech"],"exercises":[{"question":"Last night, we ___ a film.","options":["watch","watched","have watched"],"answer":"watched","explanation":"Use the Past Simple for a completed past event."},{"question":"Did he ___ the answer?","options":["knew","know","known"],"answer":"know","explanation":"After did, use the base form."}]},{"slug":"pronouns-determiners","title":"Pronouns and Possessive Determiners","language":"en","grades":[5,6],"summary":"Subject pronouns perform the action (I, you, he, she, it, we, they).","explanation":"Subject pronouns perform the action (I, you, he, she, it, we, they). Object pronouns receive the action or follow a preposition (me, you, him, her, it, us, them). Possessive determiners come before a noun (my, your, his, her, its, our, their), while possessive pronouns stand alone (mine, yours, his, hers, ours, theirs). These forms are not interchangeable.","formation":["Subject: She knows Tom.","Object: Tom knows her.","Possessive determiner + noun: This is my bag.","Possessive pronoun alone: This bag is mine."],"usage":["Avoid repeating names when the person or thing is clear.","Show ownership and relationships."],"examples":[{"sentence":"They invited us.","note":"Us is the object pronoun."},{"sentence":"Their house is large.","note":"Their comes before a noun."},{"sentence":"That seat is ours.","note":"Ours stands alone."}],"exceptions":["Its shows possession; it\'s means it is or it has.","You is both subject and object in modern English."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: Me like it. Correct: I like it.","Incorrect: This is her\'s. Correct: This is hers."],"related_topics":["articles-plurals","have-got"],"exercises":[{"question":"Alex and ___ are friends.","options":["I","me","my"],"answer":"I","explanation":"Use a subject pronoun as the subject."},{"question":"This is ___ jacket.","options":["mine","my","me"],"answer":"my","explanation":"Use a possessive determiner before a noun."}]},{"slug":"comparatives-superlatives","title":"Comparatives and Superlatives","language":"en","grades":[6,7],"summary":"Use comparative forms to compare two people or things and superlative forms to identify the highest or lowest degree in a group.","explanation":"Use comparative forms to compare two people or things and superlative forms to identify the highest or lowest degree in a group. Short adjectives usually take -er and -est. Longer adjectives often use more and most. Some forms are irregular. Comparatives are commonly followed by than; superlatives usually use the.","formation":["Short adjective: tall → taller → the tallest.","Adjective ending in -y: happy → happier → the happiest.","Long adjective: interesting → more interesting → the most interesting.","Irregular: good → better → the best; bad → worse → the worst."],"usage":["Compare size, ability, quality, cost or experience.","Use than after many comparatives; use the before superlatives."],"examples":[{"sentence":"This test is easier than the last one.","note":"A comparative compares two tests."},{"sentence":"It is the most exciting ride in the park.","note":"A superlative compares one item with a group."},{"sentence":"Today is better than yesterday.","note":"Good has an irregular comparative."}],"exceptions":["Some two-syllable adjectives accept either pattern depending on usage, such as cleverer/more clever.","Use as + adjective + as for equality: as tall as."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: more easier. Correct: easier.","Incorrect: the most fast. Correct: the fastest."],"related_topics":["adverbs-of-frequency","countable-uncountable"],"exercises":[{"question":"My bag is ___ than yours.","options":["heavy","heavier","heaviest"],"answer":"heavier","explanation":"Use the comparative form with than."},{"question":"That was ___ day of the year.","options":["the coldest","colder","cold"],"answer":"the coldest","explanation":"Use a superlative for the highest degree in a group."}]},{"slug":"countable-uncountable","title":"Countable and Uncountable Nouns: Some and Any","language":"en","grades":[6,7],"summary":"Countable nouns can be counted and have singular and plural forms (one apple, two apples).","explanation":"Countable nouns can be counted and have singular and plural forms (one apple, two apples). Uncountable nouns are treated as a mass or concept and normally do not take a plural -s (water, information, advice). Use some commonly in affirmative statements and any in many questions and negatives. Use much with uncountable nouns and many with plural countable nouns.","formation":["Countable: a/an + singular noun; many/few + plural noun.","Uncountable: some water; much information; a little time.","Some in offers and requests: Would you like some tea?","Any in many questions and negatives: Are there any eggs? / There isn\'t any milk."],"usage":["Talk about quantities, food, objects and abstract concepts.","Choose a quantifier that matches the noun type."],"examples":[{"sentence":"There are some apples on the table.","note":"Apples is countable and plural."},{"sentence":"We don\'t have any milk.","note":"Milk is uncountable."},{"sentence":"How much advice did she give you?","note":"Advice is uncountable in English."}],"exceptions":["Use a piece of advice / a piece of information to count these nouns.","Some is normal in polite offers and requests even when the sentence is a question."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: an advice. Correct: some advice / a piece of advice.","Incorrect: many money. Correct: much money."],"related_topics":["articles-plurals","comparatives-superlatives"],"exercises":[{"question":"How ___ books do you need?","options":["much","many","any"],"answer":"many","explanation":"Books is a plural countable noun."},{"question":"Could I have ___ water, please?","options":["some","many","a"],"answer":"some","explanation":"Some is natural in polite requests."}]},{"slug":"modal-can-must-have-to","title":"Modal Verbs: Can, Must and Have to","language":"en","grades":[6,7],"summary":"Modal verbs express ability, permission, possibility or obligation.","explanation":"Modal verbs express ability, permission, possibility or obligation. Can is used for ability and informal permission. Must expresses strong obligation or the speaker\'s authority. Have to expresses a requirement or necessity, often imposed by circumstances or rules. A modal is followed by the base form of the verb without to; have to is an exception because have is followed by to + verb.","formation":["can/can\'t + base verb: She can swim.","must/mustn\'t + base verb: You must listen; you mustn\'t touch it.","have/has to + base verb; don\'t/doesn\'t have to for no necessity.","Questions: Can you...? Do you have to...?"],"usage":["Ability, permission, rules, obligation and lack of necessity.","Choose carefully between prohibition and no obligation."],"examples":[{"sentence":"You mustn\'t use your phone here.","note":"Mustn\'t means it is prohibited."},{"sentence":"You don\'t have to come early.","note":"It is not necessary, but it is allowed."},{"sentence":"Can I borrow your pen?","note":"Can asks for permission informally."}],"exceptions":["Must not and don\'t have to do not mean the same thing.","Must usually has no -s: He must go, not he musts go."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: She can to swim. Correct: She can swim.","Incorrect: You don\'t have to park here (when meaning prohibition). Use mustn\'t for prohibition."],"related_topics":["present-simple","modal-verbs-advice"],"exercises":[{"question":"You ___ wear a seat belt. It is the law.","options":["must","can to","must to"],"answer":"must","explanation":"Must expresses a strong obligation."},{"question":"You ___ bring food; lunch is provided.","options":["mustn\'t","don\'t have to","can\'t to"],"answer":"don\'t have to","explanation":"Don\'t have to means there is no need."}]},{"slug":"past-progressive","title":"Past Progressive / Past Continuous","language":"en","grades":[7,8],"summary":"The Past Progressive describes an action that was in progress at a particular moment in the past.","explanation":"The Past Progressive describes an action that was in progress at a particular moment in the past. It is formed with was/were + verb-ing. It often provides background information in a story or describes an action that was interrupted by another event in the Past Simple.","formation":["Affirmative: was/were + verb-ing.","Negative: wasn\'t/weren\'t + verb-ing.","Question: Was/Were + subject + verb-ing?"],"usage":["An action in progress at a stated past time.","Background actions in narratives.","Two actions happening at the same time; one longer action interrupted by a shorter one."],"examples":[{"sentence":"At eight, I was doing my homework.","note":"The action was in progress at eight."},{"sentence":"We were walking home when it started to rain.","note":"The longer action is Past Progressive; the interruption is Past Simple."},{"sentence":"While I was cooking, Sam was setting the table.","note":"Two actions were in progress together."}],"exceptions":["Stative verbs are not normally progressive in their usual meanings.","While often introduces a longer action, but context determines the tense."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: They was sleeping. Correct: They were sleeping.","Incorrect: I was fell. Correct: I fell / I was falling, depending on meaning."],"related_topics":["past-simple","past-simple-vs-progressive","past-perfect"],"exercises":[{"question":"At 9 p.m., she ___ a book.","options":["read","was reading","has read"],"answer":"was reading","explanation":"The action was in progress at a past time."},{"question":"They ___ when the lights went out.","options":["were dancing","danced","have danced"],"answer":"were dancing","explanation":"The ongoing action provides the background to the interruption."}]},{"slug":"past-simple-vs-progressive","title":"Past Simple vs Past Progressive","language":"en","grades":[7,8],"summary":"Use the Past Simple for a completed event or a sequence of completed actions.","explanation":"Use the Past Simple for a completed event or a sequence of completed actions. Use the Past Progressive for an action in progress around a particular past moment. In narratives, the Past Progressive often gives background and the Past Simple moves the story forward. Both tenses can occur in one sentence when they describe different aspects of the past.","formation":["Completed event: subject + Past Simple.","Ongoing background: was/were + verb-ing.","Common pattern: Past Progressive + when + Past Simple; or While + Past Progressive, Past Simple."],"usage":["Tell stories and explain what was happening when another event occurred.","Distinguish the event itself from its background."],"examples":[{"sentence":"I was cycling when I saw the accident.","note":"Cycling was in progress; seeing the accident is the event."},{"sentence":"She opened the door, walked in and sat down.","note":"A sequence of completed events."},{"sentence":"While we were waiting, it began to snow.","note":"Waiting is the background action."}],"exceptions":["When does not always require Past Simple; it depends on the meaning of each action.","Some verbs describe states and are usually not used in progressive forms."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: While I cooked, the phone was ringing (if both should be ongoing). Better: While I was cooking, the phone rang.","Incorrect: I was seeing him yesterday (for a single meeting). Better: I saw him yesterday."],"related_topics":["past-simple","past-progressive"],"exercises":[{"question":"I ___ dinner when the phone rang.","options":["made","was making","have made"],"answer":"was making","explanation":"Dinner-making was in progress when the phone rang."},{"question":"The bus ___ at seven and left immediately.","options":["arrived","was arriving","has arrived"],"answer":"arrived","explanation":"A completed event in a sequence takes the Past Simple."}]},{"slug":"going-to-future","title":"Be going to","language":"en","grades":[7,8],"summary":"Be going to is used for intentions and plans already made before speaking.","explanation":"Be going to is used for intentions and plans already made before speaking. It is also used for predictions based on present evidence. The correct form of be changes with the subject, followed by going to and the base form of the main verb.","formation":["Affirmative: subject + am/is/are + going to + base verb.","Negative: be + not + going to + base verb.","Question: Am/Is/Are + subject + going to + base verb?"],"usage":["Plans and intentions: I am going to study tonight.","Predictions based on visible evidence: Look at those clouds; it is going to rain."],"examples":[{"sentence":"We are going to visit our grandparents on Sunday.","note":"A plan made before speaking."},{"sentence":"Be careful! You are going to drop that glass.","note":"A prediction based on evidence."}],"exceptions":["Going to can be used for a planned future, but present progressive is also common for fixed arrangements.","The verb after going to stays in the base form."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: She going to leave. Correct: She is going to leave.","Incorrect: They are going to visiting us. Correct: They are going to visit us."],"related_topics":["will-future","future-forms"],"exercises":[{"question":"Look at the sky! It ___ rain.","options":["is going to","goes to","will to"],"answer":"is going to","explanation":"Use be going to for an evidence-based prediction."},{"question":"I ___ learn to drive next year; I have already planned it.","options":["am going to","going to","am go to"],"answer":"am going to","explanation":"An intention needs the correct form of be."}]},{"slug":"will-future","title":"Will Future","language":"en","grades":[7,8],"summary":"Use will + the base form for predictions based on opinion, spontaneous decisions, offers, promises and willingness.","explanation":"Use will + the base form for predictions based on opinion, spontaneous decisions, offers, promises and willingness. The negative is will not or won\'t. In ordinary future clauses introduced by when, as soon as or if, English usually uses a present tense rather than will for the future time clause.","formation":["Affirmative: subject + will + base verb.","Negative: subject + won\'t + base verb.","Question: Will + subject + base verb?","Short form: I\'ll, you\'ll, he\'ll; won\'t is the negative form."],"usage":["Predictions and opinions about the future.","Decisions made at the moment of speaking.","Promises, offers and requests."],"examples":[{"sentence":"I think our team will win.","note":"A prediction or opinion."},{"sentence":"The phone is ringing. I\'ll answer it.","note":"A spontaneous decision."},{"sentence":"I\'ll help you with your bags.","note":"An offer."}],"exceptions":["Use the Present Simple in future time clauses: I\'ll call you when I arrive.","Will can also express willingness, not only future time."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: She will goes. Correct: She will go.","Incorrect: I\'ll call you when I will arrive. Correct: I\'ll call you when I arrive."],"related_topics":["going-to-future","future-forms","first-conditional"],"exercises":[{"question":"I promise I ___ tell anyone.","options":["won\'t","don\'t will","will not to"],"answer":"won\'t","explanation":"Will/won\'t is followed by the base verb."},{"question":"I\'ll wait here until the bus ___.","options":["will come","comes","coming"],"answer":"comes","explanation":"Use Present Simple in a future time clause."}]},{"slug":"present-perfect-intro","title":"Present Perfect: Introduction","language":"en","grades":[7,8],"summary":"The Present Perfect connects the past with the present.","explanation":"The Present Perfect connects the past with the present. It is formed with have/has + past participle. It can describe life experiences without stating exactly when they happened, recent events with a present result, or situations that began in the past and continue now. The past participle is the third form of the verb and may be regular or irregular.","formation":["Affirmative: have/has + past participle.","Negative: haven\'t/hasn\'t + past participle.","Question: Have/Has + subject + past participle?","Regular: play/played/played; irregular: go/went/gone."],"usage":["Life experiences: I have visited Ireland.","Present results: She has lost her keys.","Actions or states continuing to now, often with for/since."],"examples":[{"sentence":"Have you ever tried sushi?","note":"An experience; the exact time is not given."},{"sentence":"He has broken his glasses, so he cannot see well.","note":"A past event has a present result."}],"exceptions":["Do not normally use the Present Perfect with a finished past-time expression such as yesterday or in 2020.","British and American usage can differ in some recent-event contexts."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: I have seen him yesterday. Correct: I saw him yesterday.","Incorrect: She has went. Correct: She has gone."],"related_topics":["past-simple","present-perfect-vs-past-simple","since-for-already-yet-just"],"exercises":[{"question":"I have never ___ a horse.","options":["ride","rode","ridden"],"answer":"ridden","explanation":"Present Perfect uses the past participle."},{"question":"___ you ever been to Scotland?","options":["Did","Have","Were"],"answer":"Have","explanation":"Use have + past participle for life experiences."}]},{"slug":"first-conditional","title":"First Conditional","language":"en","grades":[7,8],"summary":"The First Conditional describes a real or possible future situation and its likely result.","explanation":"The First Conditional describes a real or possible future situation and its likely result. The if-clause usually uses the Present Simple, while the main clause uses will + base verb. The clauses can change order. A comma is normally used when the if-clause comes first.","formation":["If + Present Simple, will + base verb.","If you study, you will improve.","Result first: You will improve if you study (usually no comma).","Other result forms can include can, may or an imperative."],"usage":["Talk about realistic future possibilities and their consequences.","Give warnings, advice and plans depending on a condition."],"examples":[{"sentence":"If it rains, we will stay indoors.","note":"The rain is a possible future condition."},{"sentence":"If you finish early, you can help me.","note":"Can expresses a possible result."}],"exceptions":["Do not normally put will directly in the if-clause: If I see her, I\'ll tell her.","Unless means if not in many contexts."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: If it will rain, we will stay home. Correct: If it rains, we will stay home.","Incorrect: If you will study, you pass. Correct: If you study, you will pass."],"related_topics":["will-future","second-conditional","complex-conditionals"],"exercises":[{"question":"If she ___ now, she will catch the train.","options":["leaves","will leave","left"],"answer":"leaves","explanation":"Use Present Simple in the if-clause."},{"question":"If you are tired, ___ a break.","options":["take","will taking","took"],"answer":"take","explanation":"An imperative can express the result or advice."}]},{"slug":"present-perfect-vs-past-simple","title":"Present Perfect vs Past Simple","language":"en","grades":[8,9],"summary":"The Past Simple is used for completed events linked to a finished past time.","explanation":"The Past Simple is used for completed events linked to a finished past time. The Present Perfect connects an earlier event or situation to the present, and the exact finished time is not the focus. Ask whether the time period is finished and whether the sentence names a specific past time. If it does, Past Simple is normally required.","formation":["Past Simple: subject + past form; use with yesterday, last week, in 2022.","Present Perfect: have/has + past participle; often with ever, never, already, yet or unfinished time periods.","Compare: I saw the film last night. / I have seen the film twice."],"usage":["Choose a tense for experiences, recent results, finished events and time periods.","Make the connection to the present clear."],"examples":[{"sentence":"We went to Rome in April.","note":"April is a specified finished time."},{"sentence":"We have been to Rome three times.","note":"The number of experiences matters, not the exact dates."},{"sentence":"Have you finished your homework yet?","note":"Yet asks whether something has happened by now."}],"exceptions":["An unfinished time period such as today or this week can take Present Perfect if the period is still open.","American English often uses Past Simple in some contexts where British English prefers Present Perfect."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: She has called me two hours ago. Correct: She called me two hours ago.","Incorrect: Did you ever see snow? may be possible in some varieties; Have you ever seen snow? is the standard experience question taught here."],"related_topics":["present-perfect-intro","since-for-already-yet-just","past-simple"],"exercises":[{"question":"I ___ my keys yesterday.","options":["have lost","lost","lose"],"answer":"lost","explanation":"Yesterday is a finished past-time expression."},{"question":"___ you ever eaten Thai food?","options":["Did","Have","Were"],"answer":"Have","explanation":"Use Present Perfect for life experience without a specified finished time."}]},{"slug":"since-for-already-yet-just","title":"Since, For, Already, Yet and Just","language":"en","grades":[8,9],"summary":"Since marks the starting point of a period, while for tells us its duration.","explanation":"Since marks the starting point of a period, while for tells us its duration. Both frequently occur with the Present Perfect for situations that continue to the present. Already means sooner than expected or by now; yet is common in questions and negatives about whether something has happened; just means a very short time ago. Position in the sentence matters.","formation":["since + starting point: since Monday, since 2021, since I was ten.","for + duration: for two hours, for a long time.","already usually before the past participle: has already finished.","yet usually at the end of a question/negative: Have you eaten yet?","just usually before the past participle: has just arrived."],"usage":["State how long something has continued.","Ask whether an expected event has happened or report a recent event."],"examples":[{"sentence":"I have lived here for three years.","note":"Three years is a duration."},{"sentence":"I have lived here since 2023.","note":"2023 is the starting point."},{"sentence":"She has just left.","note":"She left a very short time ago."}],"exceptions":["For can also be used with other tenses; the Present Perfect is not automatic.","Already can appear in different positions for emphasis."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: since five years. Correct: for five years.","Incorrect: I haven\'t yet finished? This is possible but formal; the common pattern is I haven\'t finished yet."],"related_topics":["present-perfect-intro","present-perfect-vs-past-simple"],"exercises":[{"question":"We have known each other ___ 2020.","options":["for","since","yet"],"answer":"since","explanation":"Since introduces a starting point."},{"question":"Has the train arrived ___?","options":["already","yet","since"],"answer":"yet","explanation":"Yet is common at the end of questions asking whether something has happened."}]},{"slug":"relative-clauses","title":"Relative Clauses","language":"en","grades":[8,9],"summary":"Relative clauses add information about a noun.","explanation":"Relative clauses add information about a noun. Use who for people, which for things and animals, and that for people or things in many defining clauses. Whose shows possession, while where and when refer to places and times. A defining relative clause identifies which person or thing we mean and has no commas. A non-defining clause adds extra information and is separated by commas.","formation":["Noun + who/which/that + clause.","Possession: whose + noun.","Place/time: where / when.","Defining: The book that I borrowed is excellent.","Non-defining: My aunt, who lives in Leeds, is a doctor."],"usage":["Identify a person or thing more precisely.","Add essential or extra information to sentences."],"examples":[{"sentence":"The girl who won the race is my cousin.","note":"Who refers to a person and identifies the girl."},{"sentence":"This is the café where we met.","note":"Where refers to a place."},{"sentence":"Mr Green, whose son is in my class, is our neighbour.","note":"Whose expresses possession."}],"exceptions":["In defining clauses, the relative pronoun can be omitted when it is the object: The film (that) we watched.","Do not use that in a non-defining clause."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: My mum, that is a teacher, ... Correct: My mum, who is a teacher, ...","Incorrect: the man which lives here. Correct: the man who lives here."],"related_topics":["pronouns-determiners","reduced-relative-clauses"],"exercises":[{"question":"The woman ___ called you is my teacher.","options":["who","where","whose"],"answer":"who","explanation":"Use who for a person as the subject of the relative clause."},{"question":"Paris, ___ I visited last year, was beautiful.","options":["that","which","where"],"answer":"which","explanation":"Use which for a thing in a non-defining clause; do not use that with commas."}]},{"slug":"passive-present-past","title":"Passive Voice: Present and Past","language":"en","grades":[8,9],"summary":"The passive voice focuses on the action or the receiver rather than the person who performs the action.","explanation":"The passive voice focuses on the action or the receiver rather than the person who performs the action. Form it with the correct form of be + past participle. The object of an active sentence often becomes the subject of the passive sentence. Use by + agent only when the person or thing doing the action is important or not obvious.","formation":["Present Simple passive: am/is/are + past participle.","Past Simple passive: was/were + past participle.","Active: People make these shoes in Italy.","Passive: These shoes are made in Italy."],"usage":["When the agent is unknown or unimportant.","In formal, factual or process-focused writing.","Describe products, rules, discoveries and events."],"examples":[{"sentence":"English is spoken in many countries.","note":"The focus is on English and the general fact."},{"sentence":"The bridge was built in 1890.","note":"The completed action and its result are in focus."}],"exceptions":["Only verbs that take an object can normally form a passive in this way.","The past participle, not the Past Simple form, follows be."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: The window was broke. Correct: The window was broken.","Incorrect: The cake made yesterday. Correct: The cake was made yesterday."],"related_topics":["passive-across-tenses","reported-speech"],"exercises":[{"question":"These cars ___ in Germany.","options":["make","are made","are making"],"answer":"are made","explanation":"Use am/is/are + past participle for the Present Simple passive."},{"question":"The old school ___ in 1920.","options":["built","was built","has build"],"answer":"was built","explanation":"Use was/were + past participle for the Past Simple passive."}]},{"slug":"gerunds-infinitives","title":"Gerunds and Infinitives","language":"en","grades":[8,9,10],"summary":"A gerund is the -ing form of a verb used like a noun.","explanation":"A gerund is the -ing form of a verb used like a noun. An infinitive is usually to + base verb. Some verbs are followed by a gerund, some by an infinitive, and some can take both with a change in meaning. After prepositions, use a gerund. Learn common verb patterns as part of the vocabulary of each verb.","formation":["Gerund: enjoy reading; avoid making mistakes.","Infinitive: want to leave; decide to study.","After prepositions: interested in learning; good at swimming.","Verb + object + infinitive: want someone to help."],"usage":["Talk about activities, preferences, plans and intentions.","Build accurate verb combinations after common verbs and prepositions."],"examples":[{"sentence":"I enjoy playing chess.","note":"Enjoy is followed by a gerund."},{"sentence":"They decided to take the train.","note":"Decide is followed by an infinitive."},{"sentence":"She is interested in learning Japanese.","note":"A gerund follows the preposition in."}],"exceptions":["Stop doing means end an activity; stop to do means pause one activity in order to do another.","Remember doing refers to a memory; remember to do refers to not forgetting a task."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: I enjoy to swim. Correct: I enjoy swimming.","Incorrect: She suggested to go. Correct: She suggested going."],"related_topics":["modal-can-must-have-to","complex-gerunds-infinitives"],"exercises":[{"question":"They hope ___ us soon.","options":["seeing","to see","see"],"answer":"to see","explanation":"Hope is normally followed by a to-infinitive."},{"question":"He is good at ___.","options":["cook","to cook","cooking"],"answer":"cooking","explanation":"Use a gerund after a preposition."}]},{"slug":"modal-verbs-advice","title":"Modals for Obligation, Advice and Permission","language":"en","grades":[8,9],"summary":"Modal and semi-modal expressions communicate obligation, advice, permission and prohibition.","explanation":"Modal and semi-modal expressions communicate obligation, advice, permission and prohibition. Must and have to express obligation; should gives advice; mustn\'t expresses prohibition; don\'t have to expresses a lack of necessity. May and could can make requests or ask for permission more politely than can. The choice depends on the force and social context of the message.","formation":["should/shouldn\'t + base verb.","must/mustn\'t + base verb.","have/has to + base verb; don\'t/doesn\'t have to + base verb.","Could/May I + base verb? for polite permission requests."],"usage":["Give advice and recommendations.","Explain rules, requirements, prohibitions and optional actions.","Make polite requests."],"examples":[{"sentence":"You should get more sleep.","note":"Advice, not a strict rule."},{"sentence":"Visitors mustn\'t feed the animals.","note":"A prohibition."},{"sentence":"Could I ask you a question?","note":"A polite request."}],"exceptions":["Mustn\'t means forbidden; don\'t have to means optional.","Should is normally followed by the base form without to."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: You should to rest. Correct: You should rest.","Incorrect: You mustn\'t come if you are busy (when meaning optional). Use you don\'t have to come."],"related_topics":["modal-can-must-have-to","modal-perfect-forms"],"exercises":[{"question":"You look exhausted. You ___ go to bed earlier.","options":["should","mustn\'t","don\'t have"],"answer":"should","explanation":"Should is used to give advice."},{"question":"You ___ take photos here; it is prohibited.","options":["don\'t have to","mustn\'t","should"],"answer":"mustn\'t","explanation":"Mustn\'t expresses prohibition."}]},{"slug":"second-conditional","title":"Second Conditional","language":"en","grades":[8,9],"summary":"The Second Conditional describes an unreal, imaginary or unlikely present or future situation and its imagined result.","explanation":"The Second Conditional describes an unreal, imaginary or unlikely present or future situation and its imagined result. Use the Past Simple in the if-clause and would + base verb in the main clause. The past form signals distance from reality; it does not necessarily refer to past time. Were is traditionally used with all subjects in formal hypothetical clauses, especially in \'If I were you\'.","formation":["If + Past Simple, would + base verb.","If I had more time, I would learn another language.","The main clause can come first: I would travel more if I had more money."],"usage":["Imagine unreal or unlikely situations.","Give hypothetical advice and discuss possibilities that are not real now."],"examples":[{"sentence":"If I were you, I would apologise.","note":"A common structure for advice."},{"sentence":"She would buy a house if she earned more.","note":"The condition is hypothetical."}],"exceptions":["In informal speech, was is also used with I/he/she in some varieties, but were is preferred in formal hypotheticals.","Could can replace would when the result refers to ability or possibility."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: If I would have money, ... Correct: If I had money, ...","Incorrect: If he studied, he will pass (when meaning hypothetical). Better: If he studied, he would pass."],"related_topics":["first-conditional","third-conditional","complex-conditionals"],"exercises":[{"question":"If I ___ the answer, I would tell you.","options":["know","knew","will know"],"answer":"knew","explanation":"Use Past Simple in the Second Conditional if-clause."},{"question":"If she had a car, she ___ to school.","options":["would drive","will drive","drove"],"answer":"would drive","explanation":"Use would + base verb for the imagined result."}]},{"slug":"past-perfect","title":"Past Perfect","language":"en","grades":[9,10],"summary":"The Past Perfect shows that one past event happened before another past event or past reference point.","explanation":"The Past Perfect shows that one past event happened before another past event or past reference point. Form it with had + past participle. It often clarifies the order of events in stories, explanations and reports. If the sequence is already clear, the Past Simple can sometimes be sufficient, but the Past Perfect makes the earlier time explicit.","formation":["Affirmative: had + past participle.","Negative: hadn\'t + past participle.","Question: Had + subject + past participle?"],"usage":["Show the earlier of two past events.","Explain a past result or reason: She was tired because she had slept badly."],"examples":[{"sentence":"When we arrived, the film had already started.","note":"The film started before we arrived."},{"sentence":"He couldn\'t enter because he had forgotten his key.","note":"Forgetting happened before the inability to enter."}],"exceptions":["Do not use Past Perfect simply because an event is in the past; use it when the earlier-past relationship matters.","Had is the same for all subjects."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: They had went home. Correct: They had gone home.","Incorrect: After she had finished, she had left (when simple sequence is intended). Often: After she had finished, she left."],"related_topics":["past-simple","past-progressive","reported-speech"],"exercises":[{"question":"By the time we arrived, the shop ___.","options":["closed","had closed","has closed"],"answer":"had closed","explanation":"The shop\'s closing happened before our arrival."},{"question":"She had never ___ sushi before that day.","options":["eat","ate","eaten"],"answer":"eaten","explanation":"Past Perfect takes had + past participle."}]},{"slug":"reported-speech","title":"Reported Speech","language":"en","grades":[9,10],"summary":"Reported speech tells someone what another person said without quoting the exact original words.","explanation":"Reported speech tells someone what another person said without quoting the exact original words. With a past reporting verb such as said, the tense often shifts backwards: Present Simple to Past Simple, will to would, and can to could. Pronouns, possessives, time expressions and place expressions may also change to fit the new speaker and context.","formation":["Direct: \'I am tired,\' she said.","Reported: She said (that) she was tired.","Present Simple → Past Simple; Present Progressive → Past Progressive; will → would; can → could.","Statements commonly use say/tell + (that) clause; tell normally needs an object: told me."],"usage":["Summarise conversations and report information.","Write news reports, stories and accounts of what people said."],"examples":[{"sentence":"\'I will call you,\' Ben said. → Ben said he would call me.","note":"Will changes to would and pronouns change with context."},{"sentence":"Mia told us that she was busy.","note":"Tell is followed by the listener/object."}],"exceptions":["Backshift may not be necessary if the information is still true or the reporting verb is present.","Say and tell have different patterns: say something; tell someone something."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: He told that he was tired. Correct: He said that he was tired / He told me that he was tired.","Incorrect: She said me. Correct: She told me / She said to me."],"related_topics":["past-perfect","advanced-reported-speech","indirect-questions"],"exercises":[{"question":"\'I like music,\' Ella said. → Ella said she ___ music.","options":["likes","liked","has liked"],"answer":"liked","explanation":"With a past reporting verb, Present Simple commonly shifts to Past Simple."},{"question":"\'I can swim,\' he said. → He said he ___ swim.","options":["can","could","will"],"answer":"could","explanation":"Can commonly changes to could in reported speech."}]},{"slug":"passive-across-tenses","title":"Passive Voice Across Tenses","language":"en","grades":[9,10,11],"summary":"The passive can be formed in several tenses by changing the form of be and keeping the past participle.","explanation":"The passive can be formed in several tenses by changing the form of be and keeping the past participle. The tense is carried by be, not by the main verb. The passive is useful when the action, result or receiver is more important than the agent. Some combinations, especially perfect continuous passives, are grammatically possible but uncommon or awkward.","formation":["Present Perfect passive: have/has been + past participle.","Future passive: will be + past participle.","Modal passive: modal + be + past participle.","Past Progressive passive: was/were being + past participle.","Example: They have repaired the road → The road has been repaired."],"usage":["Describe processes, changes, formal notices and events across time.","Focus on results rather than who performed the action."],"examples":[{"sentence":"The results will be published tomorrow.","note":"Future passive: will be + past participle."},{"sentence":"The room is being cleaned.","note":"Present Progressive passive: is being + past participle."},{"sentence":"All applications must be submitted by Friday.","note":"Modal passive: must be + past participle."}],"exceptions":["Avoid overly complicated passive forms when an active sentence is clearer.","The agent introduced by by can be omitted when unknown or unimportant."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: The road has repaired. Correct: The road has been repaired.","Incorrect: It must submitted today. Correct: It must be submitted today."],"related_topics":["passive-present-past","reported-speech","advanced-passive"],"exercises":[{"question":"The documents must ___ by noon.","options":["submit","be submitted","been submitted"],"answer":"be submitted","explanation":"After a modal, the passive uses be + past participle."},{"question":"A new bridge ___ next year.","options":["will build","will be built","is build"],"answer":"will be built","explanation":"Use will be + past participle for the Future Simple passive."}]},{"slug":"conditionals-1-2-3","title":"First, Second and Third Conditionals","language":"en","grades":[9,10],"summary":"Conditionals link a condition with a result.","explanation":"Conditionals link a condition with a result. The First Conditional describes a real future possibility (if + Present Simple, will + base verb). The Second Conditional imagines an unreal or unlikely present/future situation (if + Past Simple, would + base verb). The Third Conditional imagines a different past (if + Past Perfect, would have + past participle). The tense pattern reflects how real or remote the condition is.","formation":["First: If I study, I will pass.","Second: If I studied more, I would pass.","Third: If I had studied, I would have passed.","If-clause and result clause can change order; use a comma when the if-clause comes first."],"usage":["Discuss likely results, hypothetical situations and regrets about the past.","Choose the conditional according to the time and how real the condition is."],"examples":[{"sentence":"If it rains, we will cancel the match.","note":"A possible future condition."},{"sentence":"If I lived by the sea, I would swim every day.","note":"An unreal present situation."},{"sentence":"If she had left earlier, she would have caught the bus.","note":"An unreal past condition and result."}],"exceptions":["Unless often means if not.","Would is normally not used in the if-clause of standard conditional patterns."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: If I would have known, ... Correct: If I had known, ...","Incorrect: If I studied, I will pass (when hypothetical). Correct: If I studied, I would pass."],"related_topics":["first-conditional","second-conditional","third-conditional"],"exercises":[{"question":"If he had hurried, he ___ the train.","options":["would catch","would have caught","will catch"],"answer":"would have caught","explanation":"The Third Conditional uses would have + past participle for the imagined past result."},{"question":"If I had more time, I ___ another language.","options":["will learn","would learn","would have learned"],"answer":"would learn","explanation":"A hypothetical present situation takes the Second Conditional."}]},{"slug":"future-forms","title":"Future Forms: Will, Going to and Present Progressive","language":"en","grades":[9,10],"summary":"English has several ways to talk about the future, and they are not always interchangeable.","explanation":"English has several ways to talk about the future, and they are not always interchangeable. Will often expresses predictions, spontaneous decisions, offers or promises. Be going to often expresses prior intentions or predictions based on evidence. The Present Progressive commonly describes fixed arrangements. The Present Simple is used for timetables and in future time clauses after words such as when and before.","formation":["will + base verb: I will call.","am/is/are going to + base verb: I am going to travel.","am/is/are + verb-ing for arrangements: I\'m meeting Ana at six.","Present Simple for timetables/time clauses: The train leaves at nine; I\'ll call when I arrive."],"usage":["Choose the form that reflects intention, evidence, arrangement or timetable.","Make future plans and predictions accurately."],"examples":[{"sentence":"I think it will be a good day.","note":"An opinion-based prediction."},{"sentence":"Look at that car! It is going to crash.","note":"A prediction based on present evidence."},{"sentence":"We are flying to Dublin on Friday.","note":"A planned arrangement."}],"exceptions":["The forms can overlap; context and speaker intention determine the best choice.","Avoid will in ordinary if-clauses and future time clauses."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: I see the dentist tomorrow (if emphasising a fixed arrangement). Better: I\'m seeing the dentist tomorrow.","Incorrect: when I will arrive. Correct: when I arrive."],"related_topics":["will-future","going-to-future","present-progressive"],"exercises":[{"question":"We have booked the hotel. We ___ on Friday.","options":["are leaving","leave maybe","will to leave"],"answer":"are leaving","explanation":"The Present Progressive is natural for a fixed arrangement."},{"question":"I\'ll send you a message when I ___.","options":["will arrive","arrive","arriving"],"answer":"arrive","explanation":"Use Present Simple in a future time clause."}]},{"slug":"modal-deduction","title":"Modals of Deduction: Must, Might, May and Can\'t","language":"en","grades":[9,10],"summary":"Modal verbs can express how certain the speaker is about a conclusion.","explanation":"Modal verbs can express how certain the speaker is about a conclusion. Must expresses a strong logical conclusion, might/may/could express possibility, and can\'t expresses a conclusion that something is impossible or very unlikely. For deductions about the past, use modal + have + past participle.","formation":["Present deduction: must/might/may/could/can\'t + base verb or be.","Past deduction: must/might/may/could/can\'t + have + past participle.","Example: She must be at home. / She might have missed the bus."],"usage":["Draw conclusions from evidence.","Express certainty and uncertainty carefully."],"examples":[{"sentence":"The lights are on, so they must be home.","note":"Must expresses a strong conclusion."},{"sentence":"He might have forgotten our meeting.","note":"Might have expresses a possible past explanation."},{"sentence":"That can\'t be right; the numbers do not add up.","note":"Can\'t expresses strong disbelief or impossibility."}],"exceptions":["Must not usually means prohibition, not a negative deduction. Use can\'t for a negative deduction.","Might have does not prove the event happened; it marks uncertainty."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: He mustn\'t be at home (meaning it is impossible). Better: He can\'t be at home.","Incorrect: She might has left. Correct: She might have left."],"related_topics":["modal-verbs-advice","modal-perfect-forms"],"exercises":[{"question":"The ground is wet. It ___ have rained.","options":["must","must to","must be"],"answer":"must","explanation":"Must have + past participle expresses a strong deduction about the past."},{"question":"He is not answering; he ___ be asleep.","options":["might","must to","can to"],"answer":"might","explanation":"Might expresses a possible explanation."}]},{"slug":"third-conditional","title":"Third Conditional and Wish about the Past","language":"en","grades":[9,10],"summary":"The Third Conditional describes an unreal past condition and an imagined different result.","explanation":"The Third Conditional describes an unreal past condition and an imagined different result. It uses if + Past Perfect and would have + past participle. It often expresses regret, criticism or reflection. Wish + Past Perfect can also express regret about a past situation that cannot now be changed.","formation":["If + had + past participle, would have + past participle.","If I had left earlier, I would have caught the train.","Wish + Past Perfect: I wish I had studied harder.","Could have/might have can express possible alternative results."],"usage":["Reflect on past events and imagined alternatives.","Express regret or criticism about something that happened or did not happen."],"examples":[{"sentence":"If we had booked earlier, we would have paid less.","note":"The booking did not happen earlier, so the imagined result is different."},{"sentence":"I wish I had listened to your advice.","note":"The speaker regrets not listening."}],"exceptions":["Would have is normally in the result clause, not the if-clause.","Wish + Past Simple is used for unreal present situations, not past regrets."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: If I would have seen you, I would have said hello. Correct: If I had seen you, I would have said hello.","Incorrect: I wish I listened yesterday. Correct: I wish I had listened yesterday."],"related_topics":["conditionals-1-2-3","complex-conditionals"],"exercises":[{"question":"If she had called, I ___ her.","options":["would help","would have helped","will have helped"],"answer":"would have helped","explanation":"The Third Conditional uses would have + past participle in the result clause."},{"question":"I wish I ___ my umbrella yesterday.","options":["brought","had brought","would bring"],"answer":"had brought","explanation":"Wish + Past Perfect expresses regret about a past event."}]},{"slug":"indirect-questions","title":"Indirect Questions","language":"en","grades":[9,10],"summary":"Indirect questions sound more polite or less direct than direct questions.","explanation":"Indirect questions sound more polite or less direct than direct questions. After an opening phrase such as Could you tell me...? or Do you know...?, use statement word order: subject before verb. Do not add do/does/did inside the embedded question, although these auxiliaries may appear in a direct question. Yes/no questions often begin with if or whether.","formation":["Direct: Where does she live?","Indirect: Do you know where she lives?","Direct: Is the shop open?","Indirect: Could you tell me if/whether the shop is open?","Keep the question mark if the whole sentence is a question."],"usage":["Make requests for information more polite.","Embed a question inside another sentence."],"examples":[{"sentence":"Could you tell me what time the film starts?","note":"The embedded question uses statement word order."},{"sentence":"Do you know whether he has arrived?","note":"Whether introduces an embedded yes/no question."}],"exceptions":["If and whether are both possible in many contexts; whether is often preferred before or.","The whole sentence may be a question even though the embedded clause has statement order."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: Do you know where does he live? Correct: Do you know where he lives?","Incorrect: Can you tell me what is the time? Better: Can you tell me what the time is?"],"related_topics":["questions-word-order","reported-speech"],"exercises":[{"question":"Could you tell me where the station ___?","options":["is","is it","does it"],"answer":"is","explanation":"Indirect questions use statement word order."},{"question":"Do you know ___ the library is open?","options":["is","if","does"],"answer":"if","explanation":"Use if or whether for an embedded yes/no question."}]},{"slug":"advanced-reported-speech","title":"Advanced Reported Speech: Questions, Commands and Reporting Verbs","language":"en","grades":[10,11],"summary":"Reported speech also includes questions, commands, requests and advice.","explanation":"Reported speech also includes questions, commands, requests and advice. Reported questions use statement word order. Yes/no questions use if or whether; wh-questions keep the question word. Commands and requests commonly use tell/ask + object + to-infinitive, while negative commands use not to. Reporting verbs such as advise, promise, warn, suggest and admit have their own grammatical patterns.","formation":["Question: \'Where do you live?\' → He asked me where I lived.","Yes/no: \'Are you ready?\' → She asked if I was ready.","Command: \'Sit down.\' → He told us to sit down.","Negative command: \'Don\'t touch it.\' → She warned me not to touch it.","Suggest + gerund or that-clause: She suggested taking a taxi."],"usage":["Report conversations, instructions, requests and advice accurately.","Avoid repeating direct-speech structures inside reported clauses."],"examples":[{"sentence":"The teacher advised us to revise regularly.","note":"Advise can take object + to-infinitive."},{"sentence":"He asked whether I had seen the email.","note":"Whether introduces a reported yes/no question."},{"sentence":"She suggested that we leave early.","note":"Suggest may take a that-clause."}],"exceptions":["Suggest is not normally followed by object + to-infinitive: not \'suggested me to go\'.","Some reporting verbs take a preposition: apologise for doing, insist on doing."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: He asked me where was I. Correct: He asked me where I was.","Incorrect: She suggested me to study. Correct: She suggested studying / that I study."],"related_topics":["reported-speech","gerunds-infinitives"],"exercises":[{"question":"\'Don\'t be late!\' → She told us ___ late.","options":["to not be","not to be","don\'t be"],"answer":"not to be","explanation":"Negative reported commands use not to + base verb."},{"question":"\'Where are you going?\' → He asked me where I ___.","options":["was going","am going","did go"],"answer":"was going","explanation":"Reported questions use statement word order and often backshift."}]},{"slug":"participle-clauses","title":"Participle Clauses","language":"en","grades":[10,11],"summary":"Participle clauses use an -ing form or a past participle to make sentences more concise.","explanation":"Participle clauses use an -ing form or a past participle to make sentences more concise. An -ing clause often has an active meaning, while a past-participle clause often has a passive meaning. Perfect participle clauses with having + past participle show that one action happened before another. The understood subject should normally be the same as the subject of the main clause.","formation":["Active/simultaneous: Walking home, I met Sam.","Passive: Built in 1890, the bridge is still in use.","Earlier action: Having finished her work, she went out.","Negative: Not knowing the answer, he stayed quiet."],"usage":["Condense information in formal writing and narratives.","Show cause, time, manner or background without repeating a full clause."],"examples":[{"sentence":"Feeling tired, I went to bed early.","note":"The -ing clause gives a reason and has the same subject as I."},{"sentence":"Written in plain English, the guide is easy to follow.","note":"The past participle has a passive meaning."}],"exceptions":["Avoid dangling participles: the implied subject must logically perform or experience the participle action.","Use a full clause when the relationship between ideas would otherwise be unclear."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: Walking down the street, the rain began. Correct: Walking down the street, I got caught in the rain.","Incorrect: Having finish the task, ... Correct: Having finished the task, ..."],"related_topics":["relative-clauses","reduced-relative-clauses","nominalisation"],"exercises":[{"question":"___ all the evidence, the jury reached a decision.","options":["Considering","Considered","Having consider"],"answer":"Considering","explanation":"An -ing participle clause can introduce an active circumstance."},{"question":"___ in 1900, the building has been carefully restored.","options":["Building","Built","Having build"],"answer":"Built","explanation":"A past participle can introduce a passive description."}]},{"slug":"complex-gerunds-infinitives","title":"Complex Gerund and Infinitive Patterns","language":"en","grades":[10,11],"summary":"At higher levels, verb patterns include object + infinitive, perfect gerunds, passive infinitives and verbs whose meaning changes with the form that follows.","explanation":"At higher levels, verb patterns include object + infinitive, perfect gerunds, passive infinitives and verbs whose meaning changes with the form that follows. The correct pattern is often determined by the preceding verb or adjective. A perfect gerund (having + past participle) refers to an action that happened earlier; a passive infinitive uses to be + past participle.","formation":["Verb + object + to-infinitive: They encouraged me to apply.","Perfect gerund: He denied having taken the money.","Passive infinitive: The form needs to be signed.","Verb + -ing vs to-infinitive: remember doing / remember to do; stop doing / stop to do."],"usage":["Express actions, intentions, responsibility and sequence precisely.","Use varied and accurate patterns in essays and formal writing."],"examples":[{"sentence":"She was proud of having completed the course.","note":"The perfect gerund shows an earlier completed action."},{"sentence":"The results are expected to be announced soon.","note":"The infinitive is passive."}],"exceptions":["The object + infinitive pattern depends on the main verb: want someone to do, but suggest doing.","Perfect forms are useful when the time relationship needs emphasis, but they are not always necessary."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: They made him to apologise. Correct: They made him apologise.","Incorrect: The form needs signed. Correct: The form needs to be signed."],"related_topics":["gerunds-infinitives","participle-clauses"],"exercises":[{"question":"They persuaded her ___ for the job.","options":["apply","to apply","applying"],"answer":"to apply","explanation":"Persuade commonly takes object + to-infinitive."},{"question":"He admitted ___ the rules.","options":["to break","breaking","break"],"answer":"breaking","explanation":"Admit is normally followed by a gerund."}]},{"slug":"modal-perfect-forms","title":"Perfect Modal Verbs","language":"en","grades":[10,11],"summary":"Perfect modal structures use a modal verb + have + past participle to talk about past possibility, deduction, regret, criticism or an unreal result.","explanation":"Perfect modal structures use a modal verb + have + past participle to talk about past possibility, deduction, regret, criticism or an unreal result. Must have often expresses a strong deduction; might/could have express possibility; can\'t have expresses a negative deduction; should have expresses an expectation or criticism; would have commonly appears in unreal past results.","formation":["must/might/could/can\'t + have + past participle.","should/ought to + have + past participle.","would/could/might + have + past participle.","Example: You should have told me earlier."],"usage":["Make deductions about past events.","Talk about missed opportunities, advice after the event or imagined consequences."],"examples":[{"sentence":"She must have missed the bus.","note":"A strong conclusion about a past event."},{"sentence":"You should have checked the address.","note":"Criticism or advice about a past action."},{"sentence":"We could have won if we had practised more.","note":"An unreal past possibility."}],"exceptions":["Must have done is a deduction; had to do normally expresses past obligation.","Should have done can express that something was expected but did not happen."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: He might has forgotten. Correct: He might have forgotten.","Incorrect: You should had called. Correct: You should have called."],"related_topics":["modal-deduction","third-conditional","modal-verbs-advice"],"exercises":[{"question":"You ___ have told me; now it is too late.","options":["should","should to","had"],"answer":"should","explanation":"Should have + past participle expresses criticism or a missed obligation."},{"question":"The ground is dry, so it ___ have rained much.","options":["can\'t","mustn\'t","shouldn\'t to"],"answer":"can\'t","explanation":"Can\'t have expresses a negative deduction about the past."}]},{"slug":"mixed-conditionals","title":"Mixed Conditionals","language":"en","grades":[11,12],"summary":"Mixed conditionals combine different time references in the condition and result.","explanation":"Mixed conditionals combine different time references in the condition and result. They are used when an unreal past situation has a present result, or when an unreal present characteristic affects a past result. The tense pattern must match the intended meaning rather than a fixed formula alone.","formation":["Past condition → present result: If + Past Perfect, would + base verb.","Example: If I had taken that job, I would live in London now.","Present condition → past result: If + Past Simple, would have + past participle.","Example: If she were more organised, she wouldn\'t have missed the deadline."],"usage":["Explain how a past decision affects the present.","Discuss how a present trait or circumstance explains a past outcome."],"examples":[{"sentence":"If we had left earlier, we would be there by now.","note":"The imagined past action affects the present result."},{"sentence":"If he were more careful, he wouldn\'t have made that mistake.","note":"A present characteristic is linked to a past result."}],"exceptions":["Mixed patterns vary; choose the form that expresses the real time relationship.","Do not mechanically mix tenses without checking the intended meaning."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: If I studied harder yesterday, I would pass now. Better: If I had studied harder, I would be more confident now (depending on meaning).","Incorrect: If I would have left earlier, ... Correct: If I had left earlier, ..."],"related_topics":["conditionals-1-2-3","third-conditional"],"exercises":[{"question":"If I had accepted the offer, I ___ in Paris now.","options":["would live","would have lived","will live"],"answer":"would live","explanation":"A past unreal condition can have a present result."},{"question":"If she were more patient, she ___ the argument yesterday.","options":["would avoid","would have avoided","will have avoided"],"answer":"would have avoided","explanation":"A present characteristic can be linked to an unreal past result."}]},{"slug":"inversion-negative-adverbials","title":"Inversion after Negative Adverbials","language":"en","grades":[11,12],"summary":"Formal English sometimes places a negative or restrictive adverbial at the beginning of a sentence and inverts the subject and auxiliary for emphasis.","explanation":"Formal English sometimes places a negative or restrictive adverbial at the beginning of a sentence and inverts the subject and auxiliary for emphasis. This structure is common in formal writing, speeches and literary style. If there is no auxiliary in the original clause, do/does/did is used. The inversion is not used with every sentence-initial adverbial.","formation":["Never have I seen such a view.","Rarely does he complain.","Not only did she win, but she also broke the record.","Only after the meeting did we understand the problem.","Hardly had we arrived when it started to snow."],"usage":["Emphasise how unusual, limited or unexpected a situation is.","Create a formal or rhetorical style."],"examples":[{"sentence":"Under no circumstances should you open the door.","note":"Negative phrase first; modal before subject."},{"sentence":"Not until midnight did they finish.","note":"Do-support is used because the clause has no auxiliary."}],"exceptions":["Inversion usually occurs in the main clause after the fronted phrase, not inside an ordinary embedded clause.","Never I have seen is incorrect in this pattern; auxiliary inversion is needed."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: Rarely he visits us. Correct: Rarely does he visit us.","Incorrect: Not only she won. Correct: Not only did she win."],"related_topics":["emphasis-fronting","cleft-sentences","academic-language"],"exercises":[{"question":"Never ___ I seen such a crowded station.","options":["have","I have","did have"],"answer":"have","explanation":"After fronted never, invert have and the subject."},{"question":"Only after the call ___ we understand the problem.","options":["did","we did","have"],"answer":"did","explanation":"A fronted only-phrase triggers inversion in the main clause."}]},{"slug":"cleft-sentences","title":"Cleft Sentences for Emphasis","language":"en","grades":[11,12],"summary":"Cleft sentences divide information into two parts to emphasise a particular element.","explanation":"Cleft sentences divide information into two parts to emphasise a particular element. Common forms are it-clefts and wh-clefts. An it-cleft uses It is/was + focused element + who/that-clause. A wh-cleft begins with what, where, why or another wh-word and identifies the key information. Cleft sentences help organise contrast and emphasis in speeches and essays.","formation":["It-cleft: It was Maya who found the wallet.","Wh-cleft: What I need is a little more time.","It is the lack of evidence that concerns us.","Choose the focused element to match the intended contrast."],"usage":["Emphasise a person, place, time, reason or action.","Make contrast explicit in analytical and persuasive writing."],"examples":[{"sentence":"It was the final chapter that changed my opinion.","note":"The cleft highlights the final chapter."},{"sentence":"What matters most is how we respond.","note":"The wh-cleft presents the important point after what."}],"exceptions":["Tense agreement depends on the context; it was is common for past events.","Clefts should be used selectively, not in every sentence."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: It was Maya which found the wallet. Better: It was Maya who found the wallet.","Overusing clefts can make writing repetitive; use them where emphasis is needed."],"related_topics":["inversion-negative-adverbials","emphasis-fronting"],"exercises":[{"question":"___ I need is a quiet place to work.","options":["What","Which","It"],"answer":"What","explanation":"A wh-cleft commonly begins with what."},{"question":"It was Tom ___ solved the puzzle.","options":["which","who","where"],"answer":"who","explanation":"Use who to focus on a person."}]},{"slug":"advanced-passive","title":"Advanced Passive and Reporting Structures","language":"en","grades":[11,12],"summary":"Formal English often uses reporting passives to present claims, beliefs or expectations without naming a specific source.","explanation":"Formal English often uses reporting passives to present claims, beliefs or expectations without naming a specific source. Common patterns include It is said that..., He is believed to..., and The company is expected to.... Use the infinitive form that matches the time relationship: to do for simultaneous or later situations, to have done for an earlier action, and to be doing for an action in progress.","formation":["It is said that the building is haunted.","The building is said to be haunted.","They believe she left early → She is believed to have left early.","The team is expected to win / to be winning / to have won."],"usage":["Report opinions and claims in an impersonal, formal style.","Avoid repeating an unknown or less relevant reporting source."],"examples":[{"sentence":"The medicine is believed to reduce symptoms.","note":"The reporting passive presents a belief impersonally."},{"sentence":"He is thought to have left the country.","note":"Perfect infinitive signals an earlier action."}],"exceptions":["Use these structures only when the reporting source is general or not central.","The verb and infinitive must express the intended time relationship."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: She is said that she is talented. Correct: It is said that she is talented / She is said to be talented.","Incorrect: He is believed to leave yesterday. Correct: He is believed to have left yesterday."],"related_topics":["passive-across-tenses","reported-speech","academic-language"],"exercises":[{"question":"The painting is believed ___ in the 17th century.","options":["to create","to have been created","to be create"],"answer":"to have been created","explanation":"The perfect passive infinitive refers to an earlier action."},{"question":"It ___ that the road will reopen tomorrow.","options":["is expected","expects","is expecting"],"answer":"is expected","explanation":"Use an impersonal reporting passive."}]},{"slug":"nominalisation","title":"Nominalisation and Complex Noun Phrases","language":"en","grades":[11,12],"summary":"Nominalisation turns verbs or adjectives into nouns, for example decide → decision, analyse → analysis and effective → effectiveness.","explanation":"Nominalisation turns verbs or adjectives into nouns, for example decide → decision, analyse → analysis and effective → effectiveness. It can make formal writing concise and allow ideas to be organised around concepts rather than actions. However, too many noun-heavy phrases can make writing difficult to read. Complex noun phrases can include determiners, adjectives, prepositional phrases and relative clauses before or after the main noun.","formation":["Verb → noun: develop → development; refuse → refusal.","Adjective → noun: accurate → accuracy; effective → effectiveness.","Noun phrase: the rapid development of renewable energy.","Use articles and prepositions appropriately after nominalised forms."],"usage":["Summarise processes and abstract ideas in academic writing.","Create precise topic sentences and formal reports."],"examples":[{"sentence":"The government decided to act. → The government\'s decision to act was welcomed.","note":"Decision turns the action into a concept."},{"sentence":"The rapid expansion of the city created pressure on housing.","note":"A complex noun phrase compresses information."}],"exceptions":["Nominalisation can hide the person responsible; use active verbs when agency is important.","Word forms are not always predictable and should be learned in context."],"common_mistakes":["Overly dense: The implementation of the improvement of the system... Prefer: Implementing the system improved... when clarity matters.","Incorrect: an analyse. Correct: an analysis."],"related_topics":["participle-clauses","academic-language","cohesion-reference"],"exercises":[{"question":"The verb decide becomes the noun ___.","options":["deciding","decision","decisive"],"answer":"decision","explanation":"Decision is the noun form of decide."},{"question":"Choose the most natural phrase.","options":["the expand of the city","the city\'s expansion","the expanding city of"],"answer":"the city\'s expansion","explanation":"Expansion is a noun that expresses the process of expanding."}]},{"slug":"concession-complex-linking","title":"Concession and Complex Linking","language":"en","grades":[11,12],"summary":"Concession acknowledges an opposing fact or argument while maintaining the main point.","explanation":"Concession acknowledges an opposing fact or argument while maintaining the main point. Common conjunctions and linking phrases include although, even though, whereas, while, despite, in spite of, nevertheless and nonetheless. Although/even though introduce a clause with a subject and verb; despite/in spite of are followed by a noun phrase or gerund unless followed by the phrase the fact that.","formation":["Although + subject + verb: Although it was raining, we went out.","Despite + noun/-ing: Despite the rain, we went out.","Despite the fact that + clause.","However/nevertheless connect ideas across clauses or sentences with appropriate punctuation."],"usage":["Compare arguments fairly and show a contrast or unexpected result.","Improve cohesion in essays, reviews and discussions."],"examples":[{"sentence":"Although the plan is expensive, it could save money later.","note":"Although introduces a concessive clause."},{"sentence":"Despite being tired, she continued working.","note":"Despite is followed by a gerund phrase."},{"sentence":"The evidence is limited; nevertheless, the claim deserves attention.","note":"Nevertheless links two contrasting ideas."}],"exceptions":["Do not use although and but together in the same clause structure: Although it rained, we went out (not although it rained, but we went out).","Whereas often contrasts two facts rather than introducing an unexpected result."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: Despite it was late... Correct: Although it was late... / Despite the late hour...","Incorrect: Although it was raining, but we left. Correct: Although it was raining, we left."],"related_topics":["academic-language","cohesion-reference"],"exercises":[{"question":"___ the heavy traffic, we arrived on time.","options":["Although","Despite","Even though"],"answer":"Despite","explanation":"Despite is followed by a noun phrase."},{"question":"Although the task was difficult, she ___ it.","options":["completed","despite completed","nevertheless completed it although"],"answer":"completed","explanation":"Although introduces a clause; do not add but or another redundant conjunction."}]},{"slug":"subjunctive-mandative","title":"Subjunctive and Mandative Structures","language":"en","grades":[12],"summary":"The mandative subjunctive is used in formal English after expressions of recommendation, demand, importance or necessity.","explanation":"The mandative subjunctive is used in formal English after expressions of recommendation, demand, importance or necessity. The base form of the verb is used in a that-clause, including with third-person singular subjects. British English also commonly uses should + base verb in these clauses. The structure is especially frequent in formal, administrative and academic contexts.","formation":["It is essential that every applicant be present.","They recommended that he apply again.","The committee demanded that the report be revised.","Alternative: They recommended that he should apply again."],"usage":["Express demands, recommendations, proposals and requirements formally.","Write policies, academic arguments and official statements."],"examples":[{"sentence":"The doctor insisted that she rest for a few days.","note":"The verb after that is in the base form."},{"sentence":"It is important that the data be checked.","note":"Be is used even with a singular subject."}],"exceptions":["This mandative form is different from the hypothetical were-subjunctive in If I were you.","Usage varies between British and American English; should is common in British English."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect in this formal pattern: They demanded that he submits it. Correct: They demanded that he submit it / should submit it.","Incorrect: It is vital that she is informed (in mandative subjunctive style). Preferred: It is vital that she be informed / should be informed."],"related_topics":["academic-language","advanced-passive","complex-conditionals"],"exercises":[{"question":"The panel recommended that he ___ again.","options":["applies","apply","applied"],"answer":"apply","explanation":"The mandative subjunctive uses the base form."},{"question":"It is essential that the form ___ signed.","options":["is","be","was"],"answer":"be","explanation":"The passive mandative subjunctive uses be + past participle."}]},{"slug":"advanced-tense-aspect","title":"Advanced Tense and Aspect in Narration","language":"en","grades":[11,12],"summary":"Advanced narration uses tense and aspect to manage chronology, duration, completion and viewpoint.","explanation":"Advanced narration uses tense and aspect to manage chronology, duration, completion and viewpoint. Past Simple moves a story forward; Past Progressive creates a scene or shows an action in progress; Past Perfect marks an earlier event; Past Perfect Progressive emphasises duration before a past point. Present Perfect and Present Perfect Progressive connect past activity to the present in different ways.","formation":["Past Perfect Progressive: had been + verb-ing.","Present Perfect Progressive: have/has been + verb-ing.","Past Perfect: had + past participle.","Compare: She had worked there (completed/experience focus) vs She had been working there (duration/process focus)."],"usage":["Build clear timelines in narratives and reflective writing.","Emphasise duration, result, completion or background depending on the aspect."],"examples":[{"sentence":"By the time the train came, we had been waiting for an hour.","note":"The perfect progressive emphasises duration before a past point."},{"sentence":"I have been studying all morning, so I need a break.","note":"The activity continues or has a present result."}],"exceptions":["Stative verbs are not normally used in progressive forms for their usual meanings.","Tense choice depends on viewpoint and context, not on a signal word alone."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: I had been know her for years. Correct: I had known her for years.","Incorrect: When I arrived, he has left. Correct: When I arrived, he had left (if he left earlier)."],"related_topics":["past-perfect","past-progressive","present-perfect-vs-past-simple"],"exercises":[{"question":"They were exhausted because they ___ all day.","options":["had been travelling","travel","have travel"],"answer":"had been travelling","explanation":"Past Perfect Progressive emphasises the activity leading up to a past point."},{"question":"I ___ here since nine, and I am still waiting.","options":["have been sitting","sat","had sit"],"answer":"have been sitting","explanation":"Present Perfect Progressive connects an ongoing activity to now."}]},{"slug":"reduced-relative-clauses","title":"Reduced Relative Clauses","language":"en","grades":[11,12],"summary":"A relative clause can sometimes be shortened to a participle phrase when the relative pronoun and a form of be can be omitted, or when the relative clause has an active meaning that can be expressed with an -ing participle.","explanation":"A relative clause can sometimes be shortened to a participle phrase when the relative pronoun and a form of be can be omitted, or when the relative clause has an active meaning that can be expressed with an -ing participle. Reduced clauses are common in formal writing because they make sentences more concise. The shortened phrase must clearly refer to the correct noun.","formation":["Active: Students who live on campus → Students living on campus.","Passive: The documents that were signed yesterday → The documents signed yesterday.","With be: The person who is responsible → The person responsible.","Use a full relative clause when the reduced form sounds unclear."],"usage":["Condense descriptions and add concise detail.","Make formal prose less repetitive while keeping the reference clear."],"examples":[{"sentence":"The woman standing by the door is our head teacher.","note":"Standing by the door reduces who is standing by the door."},{"sentence":"The results published today caused debate.","note":"Published today has a passive meaning."}],"exceptions":["Not every relative clause can be reduced safely; check meaning and tense.","The reduced phrase should sit next to the noun it describes."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: The report written the researcher was clear. Correct: The report written by the researcher was clear.","Dangling: Walking to school, the rain began. Keep the implied subject clear."],"related_topics":["relative-clauses","participle-clauses","nominalisation"],"exercises":[{"question":"The students ___ in the library must be quiet.","options":["study","studying","studied by"],"answer":"studying","explanation":"An -ing participle can reduce an active relative clause."},{"question":"The book ___ yesterday is already sold out.","options":["publishing","published","was publish"],"answer":"published","explanation":"A past participle can reduce a passive relative clause."}]},{"slug":"emphasis-fronting","title":"Emphasis and Fronting","language":"en","grades":[11,12],"summary":"Fronting places an element at the beginning of a sentence to make it prominent or to connect it to previous information.","explanation":"Fronting places an element at the beginning of a sentence to make it prominent or to connect it to previous information. It can create contrast, focus or a literary effect. Fronted elements may include adverbs, complements, objects and prepositional phrases. Some fronted negative or restrictive expressions require inversion; ordinary fronting does not always do so.","formation":["Normal: I will never forget that day.","Fronted: That day, I will never forget.","Normal: We especially need clear evidence.","Fronted: Clear evidence is what we especially need.","Negative fronting: Rarely have we seen such unity (requires inversion)."],"usage":["Control information flow and connect a sentence to its context.","Emphasise contrasts and improve rhetorical style when appropriate."],"examples":[{"sentence":"Into the room came the final speaker.","note":"A literary fronting structure creates a dramatic entrance."},{"sentence":"Most important of all is the question of fairness.","note":"The complement is fronted for emphasis."}],"exceptions":["Fronting is stylistic; in neutral writing, ordinary word order is often clearer.","Negative adverbial fronting may trigger inversion, while many other fronted elements do not."],"common_mistakes":["Avoid fronting so much that the sentence becomes unnatural or hard to follow.","Do not confuse simple fronting with the required inversion after Never/Rarely/Only after."],"related_topics":["inversion-negative-adverbials","cleft-sentences","cohesion-reference"],"exercises":[{"question":"Which sentence correctly uses negative inversion?","options":["Rarely we have seen this.","Rarely have we seen this.","Rarely have seen we this."],"answer":"Rarely have we seen this.","explanation":"The auxiliary comes before the subject after fronted rarely."},{"question":"Choose the natural fronted sentence.","options":["That day, I will never forget.","That day, never I forget will.","That day, I never will forget it that."],"answer":"That day, I will never forget.","explanation":"Ordinary fronting can move an object to the beginning for emphasis."}]},{"slug":"academic-language","title":"Hedging and Academic Language","language":"en","grades":[11,12],"summary":"Academic and analytical writing often uses hedging to express claims carefully rather than making them sound more certain than the evidence allows.","explanation":"Academic and analytical writing often uses hedging to express claims carefully rather than making them sound more certain than the evidence allows. Useful forms include may, might, could, appears to, seems to, tends to, is likely to and suggests that. Strong claims are appropriate when evidence is strong, but qualified language is useful when interpreting results, discussing limitations or acknowledging alternative explanations.","formation":["Hedging modal: The results may indicate a connection.","Reporting phrase: The evidence suggests that...","Probability phrase: This is likely to be due to...","Use may/might/could + base verb.","Use appear/seem + to-infinitive: The trend appears to be increasing.","Use tend to + base verb: Older devices tend to consume more power."],"usage":["Evaluate arguments and evidence without overclaiming.","Write balanced conclusions and research-style explanations."],"examples":[{"sentence":"These findings may suggest a link between sleep and memory.","note":"May and suggest qualify the strength of the claim."},{"sentence":"The difference appears to be significant.","note":"Appears to indicates a cautious interpretation."}],"exceptions":["Do not hedge every sentence; direct statements are appropriate for well-established facts.","Avoid vague wording when precise evidence is available."],"common_mistakes":["Overstated: This proves that phones cause stress. More careful: This suggests that phone use may be associated with stress.","Incorrect: The results may indicates... Correct: The results may indicate..."],"related_topics":["nominalisation","concession-complex-linking","advanced-passive"],"exercises":[{"question":"The evidence ___ indicate a possible link.","options":["may","may to","may indicates"],"answer":"may","explanation":"A modal is followed by the base form."},{"question":"Choose the most cautious academic claim.","options":["This proves every student fails.","This may suggest a possible trend.","This is definitely always the only reason."],"answer":"This may suggest a possible trend.","explanation":"Hedging avoids claiming more than the evidence supports."}]},{"slug":"complex-conditionals","title":"Advanced Conditional Structures","language":"en","grades":[11,12],"summary":"Advanced conditional meaning can be expressed with unless, provided/providing that, as long as, even if, in case, and inverted conditional forms.","explanation":"Advanced conditional meaning can be expressed with unless, provided/providing that, as long as, even if, in case, and inverted conditional forms. These expressions differ subtly: provided that and as long as set a condition; even if means the result remains true despite a possible condition; in case describes preparation for a possible event. Formal English may omit if through inversion with had, were or should.","formation":["Provided that + clause: You can borrow it provided that you return it.","Even if + clause: I will go even if it rains.","In case + clause: Take a jacket in case it gets cold.","Inversion: Had I known, I would have helped (= If I had known...).","Should you need help, contact reception (= If you should need help...)."],"usage":["Express precise conditions, exceptions and precautions.","Use formal alternatives to if in varied writing."],"examples":[{"sentence":"You may join us as long as you arrive on time.","note":"As long as sets a condition."},{"sentence":"Had they prepared better, they might have won.","note":"Had + subject replaces if + Past Perfect in a formal conditional inversion."}],"exceptions":["In case does not simply mean if: it often describes a precaution made before a possible event.","Conditional inversion is formal and does not use do-support."],"common_mistakes":["Incorrect: Take an umbrella in case it will rain. Better: Take an umbrella in case it rains.","Incorrect: Had I would known... Correct: Had I known..."],"related_topics":["mixed-conditionals","conditionals-1-2-3","concession-complex-linking"],"exercises":[{"question":"Take some cash ___ the card machine does not work.","options":["in case","even if","unless"],"answer":"in case","explanation":"In case describes preparation for a possible event."},{"question":"___ I known about the delay, I would have left later.","options":["Had","Have","Did"],"answer":"Had","explanation":"Formal conditional inversion uses Had + subject + past participle."}]},{"slug":"cohesion-reference","title":"Cohesion, Reference and Linking in Longer Texts","language":"en","grades":[11,12],"summary":"Cohesion helps sentences and paragraphs connect logically.","explanation":"Cohesion helps sentences and paragraphs connect logically. Writers use pronouns and demonstratives to refer back to ideas, linking expressions to signal relationships, and repeated key terms or synonyms to keep a topic clear. However, reference must be unambiguous: the reader should know what this, that, they or it refers to. Linking words should express the intended relation, such as contrast, cause, result, addition or concession.","formation":["Reference: This result suggests... (make the noun clear when needed: This result...).","Cause/result: therefore, as a result, consequently.","Contrast/concession: however, nevertheless, although.","Addition: furthermore, moreover, in addition.","Use paragraph topic sentences and consistent terminology."],"usage":["Structure essays, reports, reviews and longer explanations.","Guide the reader through an argument without repetitive or ambiguous links."],"examples":[{"sentence":"The sample was small. Therefore, the findings should be treated cautiously.","note":"Therefore marks a result."},{"sentence":"The policy reduced costs. However, it created other problems.","note":"However signals contrast."},{"sentence":"This approach has two benefits: it is affordable and easy to use.","note":"This approach clearly identifies what this refers to."}],"exceptions":["Do not use consequently, however and moreover as interchangeable decorations; each has a specific meaning.","A pronoun may be grammatically correct but unclear if two possible referents are nearby."],"common_mistakes":["Ambiguous: Alex spoke to Jordan after he won. Clarify who he refers to.","Punctuation: However, the results were mixed. / The results were mixed; however, ..."],"related_topics":["concession-complex-linking","academic-language","nominalisation"],"exercises":[{"question":"The sample was too small. ___, the result is uncertain.","options":["Consequently","Nevertheless","Moreover"],"answer":"Consequently","explanation":"Consequently introduces a result."},{"question":"The plan is cheap. ___, it may be difficult to implement.","options":["However","Therefore","For example"],"answer":"However","explanation":"However signals contrast."}]}]')
for grammar_topic in GRAMMAR_TOPICS:
    grammar_topic["exercises"].extend(
        ENGLISH_EXERCISE_EXTENSIONS.get(grammar_topic["slug"], [])
    )

GRAMMAR_TOPICS.extend(FRENCH_TOPICS)
TOPICS_BY_SLUG = {topic["slug"]: topic for topic in GRAMMAR_TOPICS}
DB_PATH = Path(os.getenv("SPRACHRAUM_GRAMMAR_DB", str(Path(__file__).resolve().with_name("sprachraum_grammar.db"))))

router = APIRouter()


def now_iso() -> str:
    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat()


@contextmanager
def db_connection():
    DB_PATH.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    conn = sqlite3.connect(DB_PATH, timeout=15)
    conn.row_factory = sqlite3.Row
    try:
        conn.execute("""
            CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS grammar_progress (
                user_id TEXT NOT NULL,
                topic_slug TEXT NOT NULL,
                completed INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
                attempts INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
                best_score REAL NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
                last_score REAL NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
                last_seen_at TEXT NOT NULL,
                updated_at TEXT NOT NULL,
                PRIMARY KEY (user_id, topic_slug)
            )
        """)
        conn.commit()
        yield conn
        conn.commit()
    except Exception:
        conn.rollback()
        raise
    finally:
        conn.close()


def public_topic(topic: dict[str, Any], include_exercises: bool = True) -> dict[str, Any]:
    """Return a topic but never reveal answer keys before an attempt."""
    result = dict(topic)
    if include_exercises:
        result["exercises"] = [
            {key: value for key, value in exercise.items() if key not in {"answer", "accepted_answers"}}
            for exercise in topic.get("exercises", [])
        ]
    else:
        result.pop("exercises", None)
    return result


class ExerciseAnswer(BaseModel):
    user_id: str = Field(default="local-user", min_length=1, max_length=128)
    exercise_index: int = Field(ge=0)
    answer: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=300)


class GrammarProgressUpdate(BaseModel):
    user_id: str = Field(default="local-user", min_length=1, max_length=128)
    completed: bool = False
    score: float = Field(ge=0, le=100)


@router.get("/api/v1/grammar", tags=["Grammar"])
def list_grammar(
    grade: int | None = Query(default=None, ge=5, le=12),
    q: str | None = Query(default=None, max_length=150, description="Search grammar topics by title, explanation, examples and rules"),
    language: str = Query(default="en", min_length=2, max_length=10),
    cefr_level: str | None = Query(default=None, pattern="^(A1|A2|B1|B2|C1)$"),
    limit: int = Query(default=100, ge=1, le=200),
    offset: int = Query(default=0, ge=0),
) -> dict[str, Any]:
    language = language.casefold()
    if language not in {"en", "fr"}:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=422, detail="Supported grammar languages are 'en' and 'fr'.")
    results = []
    term = (q or "").strip().casefold()
    for topic in GRAMMAR_TOPICS:
        if topic.get("language", "en").casefold() != language.casefold():
            continue
        if grade is not None and grade not in topic.get("grades", []):
            continue
        if cefr_level and topic.get("cefr_level") != cefr_level:
            continue
        if term:
            searchable = " ".join([
                str(topic.get("title", "")),
                str(topic.get("summary", "")),
                str(topic.get("explanation", "")),
                json.dumps(topic.get("formation", []), ensure_ascii=False),
                json.dumps(topic.get("usage", []), ensure_ascii=False),
                json.dumps(topic.get("examples", []), ensure_ascii=False),
                json.dumps(topic.get("common_mistakes", []), ensure_ascii=False),
            ]).casefold()
            if term not in searchable:
                continue
        results.append(topic)
    results.sort(key=lambda item: (item.get("title", "").casefold(), item.get("slug", "")))
    total = len(results)
    page = results[offset:offset + limit]
    return {"items": [public_topic(t) for t in page], "total": total, "limit": limit, "offset": offset}


@router.get("/api/v1/grammar/stats", tags=["Grammar"])
def grammar_stats() -> dict[str, Any]:
    by_grade = {
        str(grade): sum(1 for topic in GRAMMAR_TOPICS if grade in topic.get("grades", []))
        for grade in range(5, 13)
    }
    by_language = {
        language: {
            "grammar_topics": sum(1 for topic in GRAMMAR_TOPICS if topic.get("language") == language),
            "exercises": sum(
                len(topic.get("exercises", []))
                for topic in GRAMMAR_TOPICS
                if topic.get("language") == language
            ),
        }
        for language in ("en", "fr")
    }
    return {
        "grammar_topics": len(GRAMMAR_TOPICS),
        "exercises": sum(len(t.get("exercises", [])) for t in GRAMMAR_TOPICS),
        "topics_by_grade": by_grade,
        "by_language": by_language,
    }


@router.get("/api/v1/grammar/{slug}", tags=["Grammar"])
def get_grammar(slug: str) -> dict[str, Any]:
    topic = TOPICS_BY_SLUG.get(slug)
    if topic is None:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail="Grammar topic not found")
    return public_topic(topic)


@router.post("/api/v1/grammar/{slug}/check", tags=["Grammar Exercises"])
def check_grammar_answer(slug: str, payload: ExerciseAnswer) -> dict[str, Any]:
    topic = TOPICS_BY_SLUG.get(slug)
    if topic is None:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail="Grammar topic not found")
    exercises = topic.get("exercises", [])
    if payload.exercise_index >= len(exercises):
        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail="Exercise index not found")
    exercise = exercises[payload.exercise_index]
    submitted = " ".join(payload.answer.strip().casefold().split())
    answer = " ".join(str(exercise.get("answer", "")).strip().casefold().split())
    accepted = exercise.get("accepted_answers", [])
    accepted_normalised = {" ".join(str(x).strip().casefold().split()) for x in accepted}
    correct = submitted == answer or submitted in accepted_normalised
    now = now_iso()
    score = 100.0 if correct else 0.0
    with db_connection() as conn:
        existing = conn.execute(
            "SELECT * FROM grammar_progress WHERE user_id = ? AND topic_slug = ?",
            (payload.user_id, slug),
        ).fetchone()
        attempts = (existing["attempts"] if existing else 0) + 1
        best_score = max(float(existing["best_score"] or 0), score) if existing else score
        completed = int(bool(existing and existing["completed"]) or correct)
        conn.execute(
            """INSERT INTO grammar_progress
                (user_id, topic_slug, completed, attempts, best_score, last_score, last_seen_at, updated_at)
               VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)
               ON CONFLICT(user_id, topic_slug) DO UPDATE SET
                 completed=excluded.completed,
                 attempts=excluded.attempts,
                 best_score=excluded.best_score,
                 last_score=excluded.last_score,
                 last_seen_at=excluded.last_seen_at,
                 updated_at=excluded.updated_at""",
            (payload.user_id, slug, completed, attempts, best_score, score, now, now),
        )
    return {
        "correct": correct,
        "correct_answer": exercise.get("answer"),
        "explanation": exercise.get("explanation", ""),
        "attempts_for_topic": attempts,
    }


@router.post("/api/v1/grammar/{slug}/progress", tags=["Grammar Progress"])
def save_grammar_progress(slug: str, payload: GrammarProgressUpdate) -> dict[str, Any]:
    if slug not in TOPICS_BY_SLUG:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail="Grammar topic not found")
    now = now_iso()
    with db_connection() as conn:
        row = conn.execute(
            "SELECT * FROM grammar_progress WHERE user_id = ? AND topic_slug = ?",
            (payload.user_id, slug),
        ).fetchone()
        attempts = (row["attempts"] if row else 0) + 1
        best = max(float(row["best_score"] or 0), payload.score) if row else payload.score
        completed = int(payload.completed or bool(row and row["completed"]))
        conn.execute(
            """INSERT INTO grammar_progress
                (user_id, topic_slug, completed, attempts, best_score, last_score, last_seen_at, updated_at)
               VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)
               ON CONFLICT(user_id, topic_slug) DO UPDATE SET
                 completed=excluded.completed,
                 attempts=excluded.attempts,
                 best_score=excluded.best_score,
                 last_score=excluded.last_score,
                 last_seen_at=excluded.last_seen_at,
                 updated_at=excluded.updated_at""",
            (payload.user_id, slug, completed, attempts, best, payload.score, now, now),
        )
    return {
        "user_id": payload.user_id,
        "topic_slug": slug,
        "completed": bool(completed),
        "score": payload.score,
        "best_score": best,
    }


@router.get("/api/v1/grammar-progress", tags=["Grammar Progress"])
def get_progress(user_id: str = Query(default="local-user", min_length=1, max_length=128)) -> dict[str, Any]:
    with db_connection() as conn:
        rows = conn.execute(
            "SELECT * FROM grammar_progress WHERE user_id = ? ORDER BY updated_at DESC",
            (user_id,),
        ).fetchall()
    topics = []
    for row in rows:
        record = dict(row)
        topic = TOPICS_BY_SLUG.get(record["topic_slug"], {})
        record["title"] = topic.get("title", record["topic_slug"])
        record["completed"] = bool(record["completed"])
        topics.append(record)
    completed_count = sum(1 for row in topics if row["completed"])
    return {
        "user_id": user_id,
        "grammar": {
            "completed_topics": completed_count,
            "available_topics": len(GRAMMAR_TOPICS),
            "completion_percent": round(completed_count / len(GRAMMAR_TOPICS) * 100, 1) if GRAMMAR_TOPICS else 0,
            "topics": topics,
        },
    }


app = FastAPI(
    title="Sprachraum Grammar API",
    description="Grammar reference, grade filtering, examples, practice questions and grammar progress for grades 5–12.",
    version="1.0.0",
)
origins_env = os.getenv("CORS_ORIGINS", "*")
origins = [origin.strip() for origin in origins_env.split(",") if origin.strip()]
app.add_middleware(
    CORSMiddleware,
    allow_origins=origins or ["*"],
    allow_credentials=False,
    allow_methods=["GET", "POST", "OPTIONS"],
    allow_headers=["*"],
)
app.include_router(router)


@app.get("/health", tags=["System"])
def standalone_health() -> dict[str, Any]:
    """Health check for running this file as a standalone service."""
    return {
        "status": "ok",
        "service": "sprachraum-grammar-api",
        "grammar_topics": len(GRAMMAR_TOPICS),
        "exercises": sum(len(t.get("exercises", [])) for t in GRAMMAR_TOPICS),
    }
